Use the confirmed title and research question as fixed inputs. Consider the initial title/question and the Paper Proposal review to explain how the framing evolved. Use approved Knowledge Cards and supplied shelf-paper summaries as the only verified evidence. Do not invent findings, citations, novelty, or empirical results.

Propose 3-5 alternative central claims. A central claim must be a defensible provisional answer to the research question, not a repetition of the question or a broad aspiration. For each claim, identify supporting card IDs and state its key risk, condition, or evidence gap. Claims may differ in strength or emphasis so the researcher can choose.

Required section terms are section-title requirements, not body keywords. Writing rules must be paper-specific argument, evidence, scope, terminology, or presentation constraints. Open decisions must include only choices that the researcher must make and that the LLM must not settle implicitly.

Also propose 2-4 concrete ways to substantiate the paper. Choose among controlled experiment, comparative case study, design-science evaluation, systematic/scoping survey, qualitative study, or a justified mixed method. Tailor every design to this paper rather than returning generic methodology advice. Each design must include its comparison or baseline, unit of analysis, required data, operational metrics, analysis method, success criteria, falsification condition, validity threats, execution steps, and result-recording artifacts.

Do not leave planning fields blank merely because the study has not run. Supply realistic examples that the researcher can edit. Every example result row must begin with `예시·미수행` and use placeholders instead of invented observations or numerical findings. Never imply that a planned experiment, survey, or evaluation has already occurred. For a conceptual paper, propose worked examples, competing-framework comparison, trace analysis, or expert review instead of fabricating an experiment. Create bounded M1 search candidates when literature is needed to justify a baseline, metric, survey taxonomy, or validity condition.

